WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.795
This is a theatre in Oxford, mapped by a person walking it

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:04.910
with a LiDAR scanner.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.583
The white line is their path.

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:09.249
The hard part is the stairwells.

00:00:09.249 --> 00:00:11.154
Narrow, twisting, few flat surfaces.

00:00:11.154 --> 00:00:13.821
This is where most LiDAR odometry drifts.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.494
Hi, I'm Octant!

00:00:17.494 --> 00:00:21.287
Voxel-SLAM is the full mapping system from the lab behind FAST-LIO2.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:25.769
Here is how it works, and what it did on three real walks.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.585
Odometry registers each new scan against the planes in the map, with

00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:34.055
the same kind of filter FAST-LIO2 uses.

00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:38.078
Local mapping then adjusts the last ten scans together, so a scan

00:00:38.078 --> 00:00:40.796
that went in slightly wrong can still be corrected.

00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:44.598
Loop closure spots a place it has seen before and pulls the

00:00:44.598 --> 00:00:46.015
whole pose graph into agreement.

00:00:46.415 --> 00:00:49.842
And when the data ends, a bundle adjustment over the whole session

00:00:49.842 --> 00:00:51.841
lines up every wall one more time.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:57.719
Three scans of one straight wall, with the height stretched so centimetres show.

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:02.227
Scan two was registered badly earlier, and that error is already in the map.

00:01:02.647 --> 00:01:04.724
Scan-to-map registration moves only the new scan.

00:01:04.724 --> 00:01:08.285
It fits the map as it stands, so it lands on a

00:01:08.285 --> 00:01:09.769
wall that is not there.

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:14.252
Bundle adjustment moves scans two and three together, against the anchor.

00:01:14.252 --> 00:01:17.206
Each iteration refits the plane through every point.

00:01:17.626 --> 00:01:19.805
The plane never becomes a variable.

00:01:19.805 --> 00:01:23.800
Its normal is the eigenvector with the smallest eigenvalue, and that

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.253
eigenvalue is the cost.

00:01:25.673 --> 00:01:29.393
And each scan only needs three numbers per plane: a count, a

00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:31.563
sum, and a sum of outer products.

00:01:31.563 --> 00:01:33.423
The optimiser never touches a point.

00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:37.940
When a loop closes, the odometry has already moved on.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.091
Some scans left the window during the solve, and ten more

00:01:41.091 --> 00:01:42.237
are still in it.

00:01:42.617 --> 00:01:46.311
So every scan past the graph gets the last node's correction, and

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:50.004
the local map is rebuilt from them before the odometry carries on.

00:01:51.475 --> 00:01:55.562
On a walk round an Oxford college quad, the place recogniser

00:01:55.562 --> 00:01:57.048
proposed forty-five loop candidates.

00:01:57.468 --> 00:02:01.576
Twenty-nine passed the checks: a well-constrained match, and a drift under one

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:03.287
percent of the distance walked.

00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:09.108
One optimisation near the end, when the walk came back to its

00:02:09.108 --> 00:02:11.796
start, and a global bundle adjustment after it.

00:02:11.796 --> 00:02:15.157
Three point two centimetres over two hundred and eighty-three metres.

00:02:16.258 --> 00:02:20.576
Its odometry alone is six point six centimetres, a little better than

00:02:20.576 --> 00:02:22.015
FAST-LIO2's seven point two.

00:02:22.015 --> 00:02:23.815
Closing the loop halves it.

00:02:25.192 --> 00:02:29.501
In the Sheldonian stairwells, PV-LIO, which runs the same kind of plane

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:33.092
map under a filter, drifted one point seven three metres.

00:02:33.512 --> 00:02:37.772
Voxel-SLAM held it to eight point two one centimetres, with no loop at all.

00:02:37.772 --> 00:02:40.816
The global bundle adjustment at the end did the rest.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.262
The price is memory and time.

00:02:44.262 --> 00:02:48.186
It keeps every scan for loop closure and global mapping: over four

00:02:48.186 --> 00:02:49.821
gigabytes for a five-minute walk.

00:02:51.225 --> 00:02:53.399
For a map, run the full system.

00:02:53.399 --> 00:02:57.747
For a pose right now, its odometry is only a little better than FAST-LIO2.

00:02:59.608 --> 00:03:02.617
Planes, a window, loops, and a run on real walks.

00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:03.520
Thanks for watching!

00:03:04.208 --> 00:03:06.336
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:06.336 --> 00:03:06.944
I'm Octant.

00:03:06.944 --> 00:03:07.248
Bye!
